  "prediction": "**Seven Men Charged in 2014 Hazing Death, Fraternity Sues for Name Misuse**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, seven men have been charged in connection with the 2014 hazing death of University at Albany student Trevor Duffy. The men, who were allegedly part of a group that used the name Zeta Beta Tau (ZBT) Fraternity, have been accused of forcing Duffy to drink a 60-ounce bottle of vodka, leading to his death.\n\nThe fraternity, ZBT, has released a statement denouncing the group and claiming that they were not affiliated with the official Epsilon Gamma Chapter of ZBT at the University of Albany. The statement also claims that the group was a \"rogue\" group that used the ZBT name without authorization.\n\nIn response to the allegations, ZBT Fraternity has announced that it is suing the group of individuals for misusing its name and damaging its reputation. The fraternity claims that the group falsely represented themselves as members of ZBT and used its intellectual property without permission.\n\nAccording to ZBT, the name of the fraternity, its symbols, slogans, and materials are protected by United States Copyright and Trademark law. The fraternity has retained counsel to file a lawsuit against the individuals involved.\n\nThe lawsuit is seen as a novel approach by the fraternity to distance itself from the tragic event and to protect its reputation. Attorney Paul DerOhannesian, who is representing one of the students accused in connection with Duffy's death, has expressed surprise at the fraternity's decision to sue.\n\nZBT Fraternity was founded in 1898 in New York City as the world's first Jewish fraternity and has chapters on over 90 campuses across North America. The fraternity has more than 130,000 undergraduate and alumni brothers worldwide.\n\nThe case is a reminder of the dangers of hazing and the importance of protecting the reputation of legitimate organizations. The outcome of the lawsuit and the charges against the seven men will be closely watched in the coming months.",
